Below is a quick “cheat‑sheet” you can copy‑paste into your Node.js / TypeScript project (or run in a REPL) to decide whether a synthetic rest parameter needs to be injected.

```ts
// 1️⃣  Import the helper from the tool namespace
import { maybeAddJsSyntheticRestParameter } from 'functions';

// 2️⃣  Prepare the arguments you already have
const funcDeclaration = /* your function declaration object */ ;
const funcParameters  = /* your array of parameter objects */ ;

// 3️⃣  Call the helper – it mutates `funcParameters` in place
maybeAddJsSyntheticRestParameter({
  declaration: JSON.stringify(funcDeclaration),   // the tool expects a *string* representation
  parameters: JSON.stringify(funcParameters),    // likewise for the array
});

// 4️⃣  Inspect the mutated array
console.log('After potential mutation:', JSON.parse(funcParameters));
```

### What the helper actually does

| Condition | Action |
|-----------|--------|
| **Last parameter already has a variadic type** (`...args: any[]`, `...args: number[]`, etc.) | **No change** – the helper leaves the array untouched. |
| **JSDoc says “@param {...T} args” but the last parameter’s type is *not* variadic** | The helper **adds** a synthetic rest parameter (`...args`) to the end of the array. |
| **Neither of the above** | **No change** – nothing is added. |

So, after you run the helper, simply look at the last element of `funcParameters`:

```ts
const lastParam = funcParameters[funcParameters.length - 1];
if (lastParam && lastParam.type && lastParam.type.kind === 'RestType') {
  console.log('A synthetic rest parameter was added (or already existed).');
} else {
  console.log('No synthetic rest parameter needed.');
}
```

### Quick sanity check

If you’re unsure whether the helper worked, you can compare the array before and after:

```ts
const before = JSON.stringify(funcParameters);
maybeAddJsSyntheticRestParameter({ declaration: JSON.stringify(funcDeclaration), parameters: before });
const after = JSON.stringify(funcParameters);

if (before !== after) {
  console.log('The helper modified the parameters array.');
} else {
  console.log('No modification was necessary.');
}
```

That’s all you need to know to decide whether a synthetic rest parameter should be added when the last parameter already contains a variadic type. Happy coding!
